  "title": "PET-CT Osteosarcoma Study | Clinical Trials for Dogs | BVSC",
  "text": "PET-CT Osteosarcoma Study | Clinical Trials for Dogs | BVSC\nSkip to content\nReferring Vets\nRefer Online\nContact Us\nToggle Navigation\nServices\nSurgery\nOrthopaedic Surgery\nSoft Tissue Surgery\nNeurological Surgery\nMinimally Invasive Surgery\nSurgical Oncology\nInternal Medicine\nRadiation Therapy\nOncology\nOphthalmology\nPhysiotherapy\nDiagnostic Services\nI-131 Treatment\nDermatology\nBehaviour\nHyperbaric Oxygen Treatment\nEmergency Services\nFor Vets\nRefer Online\nContinuing Education\nInformation Sheets\nVisit Us\nHow to refer to us?\nPatient stories\nFor Pet Owners\nVisiting BVSC + The Animal Hospital\nVisiting BVSC + The Specialist Clinic\nYour Pet’s Stay\nCat Friendly\nGrief Counselling\nInformation Sheets\nPatient stories\nAbout Us\nAbout Us\nOur Team\nCareers\nFoundation\nPet-CT Osteosarcoma Study\nLymphosarcoma – Half Body Irradiation\nSquamous Cell Carcinoma\nProstate Cancer\nCanine Soft Tissue Sarcomas\nOur Environment\nContact\nRepeat Medications\n07 3264 9400\nPet-CT Osteosarcoma Study\nPet-CT Osteosarcoma Study\nThis is primary bone cancer and it is relatively common in dogs, especially large and giant breeds.  It is almost exactly the same as osteosarcoma in humans. We are researching some proteins in these cancer cells in dogs which may prove useful in early detection of cancer spread and help target effective treatment while reducing toxicity. For more information click here.\nOur previous research with another study identified a drug, Auranofin, which improved survival in male dogs with osteosarcoma.\nFor more information click here\n.\nAt BVSC we have completed a study in conjunction with the Centre of Advanced Imaging at the UQ using PET-CT with a novel radiolabelled peptide, 64Cu-SARTATETM, aimed to target osteosarcoma cells. In the long term, this diagnostic agent has the potential to be developed into targeted therapy for animals and humans with osteosarcoma. Positron emission tomography – computed tomography (PET-CT) is a very powerful diagnostic tool which marries a physiological study using a radio isotope with a radiographic study creating a three dimensional image profile. This shows both the primary tumour and staging.\nWe would like to thank everyone who referred cases and who was involved in this important research study, which will provide invaluable evidence in the quest to find targeted cancer therapy. The interim results have proved promising and a research paper will be written.\nFor more information, please email\nrods@bvsc.com.au\norjuliac@bvsc.com.au\n, or call Dr Rod Straw on\n(07) 3264 9400\n.\nMore information for Veterinarians and Pet Owners\nMore Information\n+ Referring Vets\nInformation on referrals, treatments, education and more.\n+ Pet Owners\nInformation about visiting BVSC, your pets stay and more.\nEstablished in 2004 by Dr Rod Straw, Brisbane Veterinary Specialist Centre is much more than just a purpose built, state of the art veterinary facility providing outstanding modern medical and surgical equipment.\nConnect with us\nQuick Links\nServices\nFor Vets\nFor Pet Owners\nCareers\nContact Us\nServices\nSurgery\nInternal Medicine\nRadiation Therapy\nOncology\nOphthalmology\nPhysiotherapy\nDiagnostic Services\nI-131 Treatment\nDermatology\nBehaviour\nHyperbaric Oxygen Treatment\nEmergency Services\nContact Info\nBVSC + THE ANIMAL HOSPITAL\n53 Old Northern Rd, Albany Creek QLD 4035, Australia\nBVSC + THE SPECIALIST CLINIC\n2/57 Old Northern Rd, Albany Creek QLD 4035, Australia\n07 3264 9400\ninfo@bvsc.com.au\n© Copyright\n| Brisbane Veterinary Specialist Centre |\nPrivacy Policy\n|\nTerms and Conditions\n|\nFind Vet Jobs\nPage load link\nGo to Top",
